{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"source .venv/bin/activate && python - <<'PY'\nfrom pypdf import PdfReader\nr=PdfReader('papers/2310.05301_brandenburg_equational_proofs_jacobson.pdf')\nfor n in range(20,31):\n    text=r.pages[n-1].extract_text() or ''\n    print('\\\\n'+'='*20+f' PAGE {n} '+'='*20)\n    print(text[:5000])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"source .venv/bin/activate && python - <<'PY'\nfrom pypdf import PdfReader\nr=PdfReader('papers/2601.12599_kinyon_machale_elementary_proofs_ring_commutativity.pdf')\nfor n in range(1,7):\n    text=r.pages[n-1].extract_text() or ''\n    print('\\\\n'+'='*20+f' PAGE {n} '+'='*20)\n    print(text[:4000])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"\n==================== PAGE 1 ====================\nIrish Math. Soc. Bulletin\nNumber 96, Winter 2025, 1–?\nISSN 0791-5578\nElementary Proofs of Ring Commutativity Theorems\nMICHAEL KINYON AND DESMOND MACHALE\nAbstract.Jacobson’s commutativity theorem says that a ring is commutative if,\nfor eachx,x n =xfor somen >1. Herstein’s generalization says that the condition\ncan be weakened tox n −xbeing central. In both theorems,nmay depend onx. In\nthis paper, in certain cases wherenis a fixed constant, we find equational proofs of\neach theorem. For the odd exponent casesn= 2k+ 1 of Jacobson’s theorem, our\nmain tool is a lemma stating that for eachx,x k is central. For Herstein’s theorem,\nwe consider the casesn= 4 andn= 8, obtaining proofs with the assistance of the\nautomated theorem proverProver9.\n1.Introduction\nN. Jacobson’s celebrated commutativity theorem for rings [14] and its generalization\nby I. N. Herstein [12] state:\nJacobson’s Theorem.LetRbe a ring such that for eachx∈R, there exists an\nintegern=n(x)>1such thatx n =x. ThenRis commutative.\nHerstein’s Theorem.LetRbe a ring such that for eachx∈R, there exists an integer\nn=n(x)>1such that[x n −x, y] = 0for ally∈R. ThenRis commutative.\nJacobson’s Theorem is a generalization of Wedderburn’s “Little” Theorem that ev-\nery finite division ring is commutative. Rings satisfying the hypothesis of Jacobson’s\nTheorem have been given various names, such aspotentrings [2, 23],J-rings [13], and\nprobably others of which we are unaware. Choosing the first one, Jacobson’s Theorem\ncan be stated succinctly aspotent rings are commutative. Rings satisfying the hypoth-\nesis of Herstein’s Theorem seem to have never been given a separate name as far as we\nknow, probably because the hypothesis is both necessary and sufficient for a ring to be\ncommutative.\nAs noted in the statements, both theorems allow the exponentnof the powerx n\nto depend onx. For Jacobson’s, there are various proofs in the literature; perhaps\nthe nicest was given independently by J. W. Wamsley [25] and T. Nagahara and H.\nTominaga [22].\nIn this paper we are interested in both theorems in the case wherenis a fixed integer\nnot depending onx, that is, ringsRfor which there exists an integern≥2 such that\nxn =xfor allx∈R. There have been various names suggested for such rings:J-rings\n[17, 18] (also used, as noted, for potent rings [13]), Jacobson rings [10],n-rings [6],\nn-potent rings [1], and, again, probably others of which we are unaware. Since elements\nxsatisfyingx n =xfor somenare often calledn-potent elements, we choose the name\nn-potentrings. We will primarily use the name in the interest of simplifying theorem\nstatements.\nThe well known class ofBooleanrings coincides with what we are calling 2-potent\nrings. Jacobson’s Theorem for such rings is a standard exercise with an easy equational\n2020Mathematics Subject Classification.16U80, 03B35.\nKey words and phrases.rings, commutativity theorems.\nReceived on 24-07-2025.\n1\narXiv:2601.12599v1  [math.RA]  18 Jan 2026\n\n==================== PAGE 2 ====================\n2 KINYON AND MACHALE\nproof (see the first alternative proof to Theorem 2.6 below). Indeed, it is a consequence\nof Birkhoff’s Completeness Theorem for Equational Logic [5] that for each fixedn, an\nequational proof of Jacobson’s Theorem exists.\nAmong the papers devoted to proofs of Jacobson’s Theorem for particular fixedn\n[8, 11, 26, 27], we would particularly like to single out thetour de forceof Y. Morita [21],\nwho gave human equational proofs for most even numbers≤50 and all odd numbers\n≤25.\nThere is certainly similar interest in equational proofs of Herstein’s Theorem for fixed\nn, although the literature is not as extensive; see, e.g., [7, 19].\nIt is natural to try to use computer assistance to generate equational proofs of either\ntheorem in the case of fixedn[24, 26, 27]. For Jacobson’s Theorem, M. Brandenburg\n[6] has recently done some very exciting work along these lines.\nThis paper is in two parts. In§2, we discuss Jacobson’s Theorem for certain cases\nof smalln. Fornodd, our main tool is a useful result we think is new (Lemma 2.4):\nifRis a(2k+ 1)-potent ring, then for allx∈R,x k is central.This result was first\nfound by Stephen Buckley (unpublished); our proof differs from his. We illustrate how\nhelpful the lemma is forn= 3,5,and 7. We also give some (we believe) new proofs for\nvarious evenn. All but one of the proofs in§2 were originally human generated; the\nproof of Lemma 2.2 was first found by an automated theorem prover and subsequently\nhumanized.\nIn§3, we turn to Herstein’s Theorem for the specific cases ofn= 2, 4, and 8.\nThe proofs were found with the assistance ofProver9, the automated theorem prover\ndeveloped by W. McCune [20]. In fact, the beginning of the authors’ collaboration was\nan email suggestion by the second author to the first that it would be interesting to\nfind automated proofs of Herstein’s Theorem forn= 4 and 8, and then to see if such\nproofs could be suitably humanized.\nWe would judge the humanization effort to be quite successful forn= 4 (Theorem\n3.7) and somewhat successful forn= 8 (Theorem 3.8). For the latter proof, although\nit is certainly possible for a patient human to follow the individual steps, the overall\npattern is difficult to see. We have no idea how or even if a general idea can be extracted\nfrom the proof which could be applied to higher powers of 2.\nWe conclude this introduction by discussing conventions. Rings are assumed to be\nassociative but not assumed to have a unity, that is, they are what some, following a\nsuggestion of Jacobson ([15], pp. 155–156), would call a “rng”.\nThecentreof a ringRis the subringZ(R) ={a∈R:ar=ra,∀r∈R}. Elements\nofZ(R) are said to becentral.\nIn our proofs, especially in§3, we will make heavy use of the commutator [x, y] =\nxy−yx. This is an interesting binary operation in its own right, but here we mainly\nuse it as a computational tool. It turns out that introducing commutators intoProver9\ninput files and feeding the program basic facts about commutators helps quite a bit in\nfinding proofs. We will discuss the commutator identities we need at the beginning of\n§3.\n2.Jacobson’s Theorem forn-potent rings\nOur main interest in this section is giving equational proofs thatn-potent rings are\ncommutative for various values ofn. However, for some preliminary results, there is\nessentially no extra work involved in giving proofs for reduced rings. A ringRis said\nto bereducedif it has no nilpotent elements. This can be equivalently described by the\ncondition\nx2 = 0 =⇒x= 0,(1)\n\n==================== PAGE 3 ====================\nRing Commutativity Theorems 3\nfor allx∈R. Every potent ringRis reduced: ifx∈Rsatisfiesx 2 = 0, letn=n(x)>1\nbe such thatx n =x. Thenx=x n =x 2xn−1 = 0. Every reduced ring is a subdirect\nproduct of domains [3, 16], but it would breach the spirit of this paper to use this rather\ndeep structural result.\nEvery reduced ringRsatisfies the condition\nxy= 0 =⇒yx= 0,(2)\nfor allx∈R. Indeed, ifxy= 0, then (yx) 2 =y(xy)x= 0 and soyx= 0 by (1). Rings\nsatisfying (2) are said to bereversible[9].\nReduced rings are neither defined nor characterized by identities. Thus even elemen-\ntary proofs in reduced rings unavoidably use (1) and hence are not, strictly speaking,\nequational. However, it is straightforward to convert such proofs to equational ones\ninn-potent rings. For example, to prove directly that ann-potent ring (n >1) is\nreversible, note that ifxy= 0, thenyx= (yx) n =y(xy) n−1x= 0.\nAn idempotente(that is, an element satisfyinge 2 =e) of a reduced ringRis central.\nThis is well known and has a short, standard proof: check that (ex−exe) 2 = 0 and\n(xe−exe) 2 = 0, soRbeing reduced impliesex−exe= 0 andxe−exe= 0, hence\nex=exe=xe. In fact, the same expressions occur in a mild generalization.\nLemma 2.1.In reversible rings, idempotents are central.\nProof.Ifeis an idempotent in a ringR, then for allx∈R,e(x−ex) = 0 and\n(x−xe)e= 0. IfRis reversible, then (2) implies (x−ex)e=xe−exe= 0 and\ne(x−xe) =ex−exe= 0. Thusxe=exe=ex, that is,eis central.□\nIn the reduced case, the proof of the following useful generalization is only a bit more\ninvolved than the classic proof and is based on the same idea.\nLemma 2.2.LetRbe a reduced ring. Ifc∈Rsatisfiesc 2 =tcfor some integert,\nthencis central.\nProof.Firstly, for allx∈R,c 2xc=tc·xc=cx·tc=cxc 2. Thusc[c, x]c=c(cx−xc)c=\n0. In particular, (c[c, x]) 2 = 0 and ([c, x]c) 2 = 0. SinceRis reduced, (1) yields both\nc[c, x] = 0 and [c, x]c= 0. These imply, respectively,\nxc[c, x] = 0 and (3)\n[c, x]cx= 0.(4)\nApplying (2) to (3), we get\n[c, x]xc= 0.(5)\nSubtracting (5) from (4), we obtain [c, x] 2 = 0. SinceRis reduced, [c, x] = 0 for all\nx∈R, that is,c∈Z(R).□\nLemma 2.3.LetRbe a reduced ring and letn >1be an integer. Ifc∈Rsatisfies\ncn =c, thenc n−1 is a central idempotent.\nProof.Ifn= 2, thenc n−1 =c, while ifn >2, thenc n−1cn−1 =c ncn−2 =cc n−2 =c n−1.\nIn either case,c n−1 is an idempotent and so Lemma 2.2 applies.□\nWe now specialize from reduced rings ton-potent rings, our real interest. The fol-\nlowing result will turn out to be crucial, and can be viewed as improving Lemma 2.3 in\nthe case of oddn.\nLemma 2.4.Letk≥1be an integer and letRbe a(2k+ 1)-potent ring. Then\nxk ∈Z(R)for allx∈R.\n\n==================== PAGE 4 ====================\n4 KINYON AND MACHALE\nProof.Firstly,x 2k is a central idempotent by Lemma 2.3. Next we showx 3k =x k. The\nclaim is clear ifk= 1, while ifk >1, thenx 3k =x 2k+1xk−1 =xx k−1 =x k. Finally,\n(x2k +x k)2 = (x2k)2 + 2x3k +x 2k = 2(x2k +x k). By Lemma 2.2 (withc=x 2k +x k,\nt= 2),x 2k +x k ∈Z(R) and sox k = (x 2k +x k)−x 2k ∈Z(R) for allx∈R, as\nclaimed. □\nForneven, we have the following.\nLemma 2.5.Letn >1be an even integer and letRbe ann-potent ring. Then2x= 0\nfor allx∈R.\nProof.For allx∈R,−x= (−x) n =x n =x.□\nFor the remainder of this section, we establish commutativity theorems forn-potent\nrings for variousn. We start with a classic, recalling that 2-potent rings are the same\nas Boolean rings.\nTheorem 2.6.Every2-potent ring is commutative.\nProof.This is the casen= 2 of Lemma 2.3.□\nAs an alternative, here is the classic proof.\nAlternative proof 1.LetRbe 2-potent. For allx, y∈R,x+y= (x+y) 2 =x 2 +xy+\nyx+y 2 =x+xy+yx+y. Cancelling, we havexy+yx= 0, and soxy=−yx=yx\nby Lemma 2.5.□\nWe also present here the stunning proof of Brandenburg [6].\nAlternative proof 2.For allx, yin a ringR,xy−yx=\n= ((x+y) 2 −(x+y))−(x 2 −x)−(y 2 −y) + ((yx) 2 −yx)−((−yx) 2 −(−yx)).\nIfRis 2-potent, then the right hand side equals 0.□\nFor a plethora of proofs and variations of our next result, see [8].\nTheorem 2.7.Every3-potent ring is commutative.\nProof.This is the casek= 1 of Lemma 2.4.□\nAlternative proof.LetRbe 3-potent. By Lemma 2.3,x 2 is central for allx∈R, so\n2x= 2x 3 = (x2 +x) 2 −x 4 −x 2 is central. Next,\nx2 +x= (x 2 +x) 3 =x 6 + 3x5 + 3x4 +x 3 =x 2 + 3x+ 3x 2 +x= 2·2(x 2 +x)∈Z(R).\nFinally,x= (x 2 +x)−x 2 ∈Z(R) for allx∈R, that is,Ris commutative.□\nLemma 2.8.Letkbe an integer and letRbe a ring in whichk(xy+yx)∈Z(R)for\nallx, y∈R. Thenkx 2 ∈Z(R)for allx∈R.\nProof.We havekx 2 ·y+kxyx=x·k(xy+yx) =k(xy+yx)·x=kxyx+y·kx 2 and\nso the desired result follows from cancelingkxyx.□\nLemma 2.9.IfRis a ring in whichx 2+x∈Z(R)for allx∈R, thenRis commutative.\nProof.For allx, y∈R, we have (x+y) 2+x+y∈Z(R), that is, (x 2+x)+(y 2+y)+xy+\nyx∈Z(R). It follows thatxy+yx∈Z(R), and thusx 2 ∈Z(R) by Lemma 2.8 (with\nk= 1). Thereforex= (x 2 +x)−x 2 ∈Z(R) for allx∈R, i.e.,Ris commutative.□\nLemma 2.10.Ifnis a power of2, then each binomial coefficient\n\u0000n\nk\n\u0001\nis even except\nfork= 0andk=n.\nProof.This is easily established by induction.□\n\n==================== PAGE 5 ====================\nRing Commutativity Theorems 5\nTheorem 2.11.Every4-potent ring is commutative.\nProof.LetRbe 4-potent. By Lemma 2.5, 2x= 0 for allx∈R. Thus (x 2 +x) 2 =\nx4 +x 2 =x 2 +xusing Lemma 2.10. By Lemma 2.2,x 2 +xis central for allx∈R, and\nsoRis commutative by Lemma 2.9.□\nTheorem 2.12.Every5-potent ring is commutative.\nProof.LetRbe 5-potent. By Lemma 2.4,x 2 ∈Z(R) for allx∈R. Thus (x 2 +x) 2 −\nx4 −x 2 = 2x 3 is central for allx∈R. Next 2x= 2x 5 = 2x 3 ·x 2 ∈Z(R) for allx.\nFinally,\nx2 +x= (x 2 +x) 5\n=x 10 + 5x9 + 10x8 + 10x7 + 5x6 +x 5\n=x 2 + 5x+ 10x 4 + 10x3 + 5x2 +x\n= 2(5x4 + 5x3 + 3x2 + 3x)∈Z(R).\nBy Lemma 2.9,Ris commutative.□\nWe will postpone the casen= 6 for now.\nTheorem 2.13.Every7-potent ring is commutative.\nProof.LetRbe 7-potent. By Lemma 2.4,x 3 ∈Z(R) for allx∈R. Thus (x 2 +x) 3 −\nx6 −x 3 = 3(x5 +x 4)∈Z(R). Multiplying byx 3, we get 3(x 2 +x)∈Z(R). It follows\nthat\n3((x+y) 2 +x+y)−3(x 2 +x)−3(y 2 +y) = 3(xy+yx)∈Z(R).\nBy Lemma 2.8 (withk= 3), 3x 2 ∈Z(R). Thus 3x= 3(x 2 +x)−3x 2 ∈Z(R) for all\nx∈R.\nNext, we have\nx2 +x= (x 2 +x) 7\n=x 14 + 7x13 + 21x12 + 35x11 + 35x10 + 21x9 + 7x8 +x 7\n=x 2 + 7x+ 21x 6 + 35x5 + 35x4 + 21x3 + 7x2 +x .\nCancellingx 2 +xfrom both sides and separating terms, we get\n0 = 3(2x+ 7x 6 + 11x5 + 11x4 + 7x3 + 2x2) +x+ 2x 5 + 2x4 +x 2 .\nThus 2x5 +2x 4 +x 2 +x∈Z(R). Replacingxin this last expression with−xand adding\ngives 4x4 + 2x2 ∈Z(R). Thusx 4 −x 2 = 4x4 + 2x2 −3(x 4 +x 2)∈Z(R)\nSince bothx 3 andx 4 −x 2 are central, so is (x 4 −x 2)2 + 2(x3)2 =x 8 +x 4 =x 2 +x 4.\nTherefore 2x2 = (x2 +x 4)−(x 4 −x 2)∈Z(R), and sox 2 = 3x2 −2x 2 ∈Z(R).\nFinally,x=x 7 =x 2 ·x 2 ·x 3 ∈Z(R) for allx∈R, and we are finished.□\nMany other cases ofx n =xfor smallncan be handled by these methods, the more\ndifficult cases being whennis odd (although Lemma 2.4 certainly helps) or a power of\n2. We believe our proofs are close to being best possible (in some sense) and would be\npleased to hear from any reader who can shorten or improve an any of them.\nWe conclude this section with one more example, covering the casesn= 6, 10, 18,\n34,. . .in a single theorem.\nTheorem 2.14.Letj≥1be an integer and assume that every2 j+1-potent ring is\ncommutative. Leti > jbe an integer and letRbe a2 i + 2j-potent ring. ThenRis\ncommutative.\n\n==================== PAGE 6 ====================\n6 KINYON AND MACHALE\nProof.We have 2x= 0 for allx∈Rby Lemma 2.5. Now\nx2 +x= (x 2 +x) 2i+2j\n= (x2 +x) 2i\n(x2 +x) 2j\n= (x2i+1\n+x 2i\n)(x2j+1\n+x 2j\n) =x 2(2i+2j ) +x 2i+2j+1\n+x 2i+1+2j\n+x 2i+2j\n=x 2 +x 2i+2j +2j\n+x 2i+2i+2j\n+x=x 2 +x 2j +1 +x 2i+1 +x ,\nusing Lemma 2.10 in the third equality. Cancelling, we havex 2j +1 +x 2i+1 = 0, that is,\nx2i+1 =x 2j +1. Finally, we have\nx=x 2i+2j\n=x 2i+1x2j −1 =x 2j +1x2j −1 =x 2j+1\nfor allx∈R. By assumption,Ris commutative.□\nCorollary 2.15.LetRbe an(2 i +2)-potent ring wherei >1. ThenRis commutative.\nProof.Takej= 1 in Theorem 2.14 and apply Theorem 2.11.□\n3.Herstein’s Theorem forn= 2,4,8\nIn a ringR, we have already used ring commutators [x, y] =xy−yxand will do so\nquite heavily in this section. Among the properties satisfied by commutators, we will\nneed the following:\n[x, x] = 0,[x, y] =−[y, x],[x, y+z] = [x, y] + [x, z]\nfor allx, y, z∈R. To improve readability, it is useful to introduce, for eachx∈R, the\nmapping ad(x) :R→Rdefined by ad(x)(y) = [x, y] for ally∈R. Properties satisfied\nby this mapping include:\nad(x)(y+z) = ad(x)(y) + ad(x)(z),\nad(x+y) = ad(x) + ad(y),\nad([x, y]) = ad(x)ad(y)−ad(y)ad(x).\nfor allx, y, z∈R. The first identity says that ad(x) is an endomorphism of the un-\nderlying abelian group (R,+). The second says that ad: (R,+)→End(R,+) is a\nhomomorphism of abelian groups. The third is where the symbol “ad” comes from; the\nidentity says that ad: (R,[·,·])→End(R,+) is theadjointrepresentation of the Lie\nring associated toR. However, for us, ad is just a notational shorthand; nothing about\na ring’s Lie ring plays a role outside of just using the identities above in calculations.\nAs discussed in§1, we are interested in ringsRsatisfying [x n −x, y] = 0 for some\nintegern >1 and allx, y∈R. We are specifically interested inn= 2,4 or 8, so we will\nstart with the broader assumption that\n[p(x)−x, y] = 0 for allx, y∈R(E)\nwherep(t) is a polynomial with integer coefficients such thatp(t) =q(t 2)t2, where\nq(t)∈Z[t].\nLemma 3.1.LetRbe a ring satisfying (E). Then2 ad(x) = 0for allx∈R.\nProof.For allx∈R,−ad(x) = ad(−x) = ad(p(−x)) = ad(p(x)) = ad(x).□\nRoughly speaking, the conclusion of Lemma 3.1 is, in the present setting, what\nreplacesRhaving characteristic 2 in then-potent setting for evenn. The conclusion\ncan be stated in various equivalent ways, such as [x, y] = [y, x] for allx, y∈R.\nLemma 3.2.LetRbe a ring such that2 ad(x) = 0for allx∈R. Then for each\nnonnegative integern,\nad(x)2n\n= ad(x2n\n).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"source .venv/bin/activate && python - <<'PY'\nfrom pypdf import PdfReader\nr=PdfReader('papers/2310.05301_brandenburg_equational_proofs_jacobson.pdf')\nfor n in range(20,31):\n    text=r.pages[n-1].extract_text() or ''\n    print('\\\\n'+'='*20+f' PAGE {n} '+'='*20)\n    print(text[:5000])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"\n==================== PAGE 20 ====================\n20 MARTIN BRANDENBURG\nThe ith summand on the left side is equal to the (i + 1)th summand on the right side. Hence,\nypx = xyp,\nshowing that yp is central. Since y + yp is central, y must be central. □\nExample 6.5. There is an equational proof that every 73-ring is commutative: The set of\np ∈ P with p − 1 | 73 − 1 is 2, 3, 5, 7, 13, 19, 37, 73, so by Theorem\n3.14 it suﬃces to prove\ncommutativity modulo these primes. We use Theorem 4.1. The exponent k is 1 for the primes\n7, 13, 19, 37, 73 and 2 for the primes 2, 3, 5. So the claim follows from Theorem 5.5 for the ﬁrst\nset of primes and from Theorem 6.4 for the second set of primes.\n7. The general case\nNext, we aim for the general case. Our methods for p-rings and p2-rings do not generalize\nto p3-rings, so that diﬀerent ideas are required. The (non-equat ional) proofs of Jacobson’s\nTheorem in [ Wa71, NT74] derive it from Wedderburn’s Theorem, which states that eve ry\nﬁnite division ring is commutative.\nMore precisely, they are structured as follows:\n(1) Reduce the general case to the case of ﬁnite rings.\n(2) Reduce the ﬁnite case to the case of division rings.\n(3) For division rings, cite (or prove again) Wedderburn’s T heorem.\nWe will follow a similar approach here, but the proofs need to be made constructive (thus,\navoiding LEM) and then transformed into equational logic. T he common proofs of the Wed-\nderburn Theorem such as the one by Witt [\nWi31] are not constructive, though. They start\nwith a ﬁnite non-commutative division ring of minimal cardi nality and derive a contradiction.\nTo solve this, we need to formulate the Wedderburn Theorem in a diﬀerent way.\nBut ﬁrst, let us observe the following interesting relations hip:\nProposition 7.1. In classical logic, the following are equivalent:\n(1) Wedderburn ’s Theorem is true.\n(2) Finite reduced rings are commutative.\n(3) For every n > 1, ﬁnite n-rings are commutative.\nProof. (1) ⇒ (2): It suﬃces to prove that every ﬁnite reduced ring A is a product of division\nrings. If A is trivial or a division ring, we are done. Otherwise (here we are using LEM) we\ncan choose a non-unit x ̸= 0 . Since A is ﬁnite, there are n, m > 0 with xn = xn+m. Then\ne := xnm is idempotent ̸= 0 , 1 and central by Lemma 2.8. Thus, A ∼\n= eA × (1 − e)A and\ninduction on the cardinality of A proves the claim. (2) ⇒ (3): This is clear since n-rings are\nreduced. (3) ⇒ (1): If D is a ﬁnite division ring, say with n elements, then D is an n-ring\nbecause of Lagrange’s Theorem applied to D× . □\nThe idea is now to formulate a constructive version of the Wed derburn Theorem for very\nspecial kinds of ﬁnite n-rings. We already know that it is enough to consider pk-rings with\np = 0 (Corollary\n4.2).\nDeﬁnition 7.2. Let p ∈ P, k ≥ 1, and f ∈ Fp[T ] be a polynomial. The constructive\nWedderburn Theorem Wp,k,f is the following statement: Let A be a pk-ring with p = 0 and\na, b ∈ A two elements with\nba = f (a)b. (7)\nThen\nba = ab.\n\n==================== PAGE 21 ====================\nEQUATIONAL PROOFS OF JACOBSON’S THEOREM 21\nThis theorem will be an intermediate step in proving commuta tivity of A. It is more easy than\njust showing ba = ab directly because of the additional assumption in eq. ( 7), which roughly\nsays that a and b “almost” commute. The statement Wp,k,T is trivial.\nLemma 7.3. The Wedderburn Theorem implies the constructive Wedderbur n Theorem Wp,k,f\nfor all p, k, f. In particular, Wp,k,f holds in classical logic.\nProof. With the notation from Deﬁnition 7.2, consider the subalgebra Fp⟨a, b⟩ ⊆ A generated\nby a, b. Deﬁne E := {aibj : 0 ≤ i, j < p k} and let M be the Fp-submodule of Fp⟨a, b⟩ generated\nby E. Then apk\n= a, bpk\n= b and eq. ( 7) imply that a ·E and b ·E are contained in M . This\nimplies E ·E ⊆ M , so that M ·M ⊆ M and therefore M = Fp⟨a, b⟩. Hence, Fp⟨a, b⟩ is a ﬁnite\npk-ring. So the claim follows from Proposition 7.1. □\nTrivially, when all pk-rings with p = 0 are commutative, Wp,k,f holds. We will prove next\nthat the converse also holds. The proof is an adaptation of th e proofs of Jacobson’s Theorem\nin [ Wa71, NT74]. The diﬀerence is that we will not use a proof by contradicti on, and indeed\nthe proof is (almost) constructive. We will use it to ﬁnd an eq uational proof afterwards.\nTheorem 7.4. Let p ∈ P and k ≥ 1. Assume that the constructive Wedderburn Theorem\nWp,k,f holds for all f ∈ Fp[T ] of degree < k . Then every pk-ring with p = 0 is commutative.\nProof. Let A be a pk-ring with p = 0, and let a ∈ A. Then Fp[a] is a ﬁnite reduced commutative\nring, hence a ﬁnite product of ﬁnite ﬁelds Fpmi by the proof of Proposition 7.1. Hence, there\nare idempotent elements ei ∈ Fp[a] such that ∑\ni ei = 1 , eiej = δi,j and such that the Fp-\nalgebra ei ·Fp[a] with multiplicative identity ei is isomorphic to Fpmi . They are pk-rings, so\nthat mi | k. Notice that ei ·Fp[a] is equal to the Fp-subalgebra of ei ·A generated by ei ·a.\nSince a = ∑\ni ei ·a, it suﬃces to prove that every ei ·a is central in ei ·A.\nTherefore, we may assume that Fp[a] is a ﬁnite ﬁeld, say Fpm with m | k. Consider the\nFp-linear map\nd : A → A, x ↦→ ax − xa.\nWe need to show d = 0, because then a is central. One proves by induction on n ∈ N that\ndn(x) =\nn∑\ni=0\n(− 1)i\n(n\ni\n)\n·an− ixai.\nApply this to n = pm and use\n(pm\ni\n)\n≡ 0 mod p for 0 < i < p m and apm\n= a. Then we get\ndpm\n= d. (8)\nNext, consider for every u ∈ Fp[a] the map\nλu : A → A, x ↦→ ux.\nIt is Fp-linear and commutes with d (since u commutes with a). In Fpm[T ] we have the\nwell-known equation\nT pm\n− T =\n∏\nu∈ Fpm\n(T − u).\nHence, the same equation holds in Fp[a][T ]:\nT pm\n− T =\n∏\nu∈ Fp[a]\n(T − u). (9)\nConsider the Fp-algebra homomorphism\nα : Fp[a][T ] → EndFp(A), u ↦→ λu, T ↦→ d.\n\n==================== PAGE 22 ====================\n22 MARTIN BRANDENBURG\nHere, EndFp refers to the Fp-algebra of Fp-module endomorphisms with ◦ as the multiplication.\nHere, α is well-deﬁned since d commutes with each λu. Applying α to eq. (\n9) yields\ndpm\n− d =\n∏\nu∈ Fp[a]\n(d − λu)\nin EndFp(A). The left side vanishes because of eq. ( 8). Therefore, we arrive at\n0 =\n∏\n0̸=u∈ Fp[a]\n(d − λu) ◦ d. (10)\nWe claim that d − λu : A → A is injective for all 0 ̸= u ∈ Fp[a]. In fact, if (d − λu)(b) = 0 ,\nthen ab − ba = ub, so that ba = (a − u)b. Since a − u ∈ Fp[a], there is a polynomial f ∈ Fp[T ]\nof degree < m ≤ k with a − u = f (a). Thus, ba = f (a)b. Since the constructive Wedderburn\nTheorem Wp,k,f holds by assumption, it follows ab = ba, which means ub = 0 . Since u ̸= 0\nand Fp[a] is a ﬁeld, u is a unit, so that b = 0, and we are done.\nSince all d − λu (u ̸= 0) are injective, eq. ( 10) implies d = 0, and a is central. □\nThe next step is to transform the proof into an equational pro of.\nTheorem 7.5. Let p ∈ P and k ≥ 1. Assume that for all f ∈ Fp[T ] of degree < k there is an\nequational proof of the constructive Wedderburn Theorem Wp,k,f . Then there is an equational\nproof that every pk-ring with p = 0 is commutative.\nProof. The ﬁrst step is to describe a product decomposition Fp[T ]/⟨T pk\n− T ⟩ ∼\n= ∏\ni Fpmi in\nmore explicit terms. Let S be the ﬁnite set of all monic irreducible polynomials over Fp whose\ndegree divides k. This can be computed with standard methods. For example, wh en p = 2\nand k = 2 we have S = {T, T + 1, T 2 + T + 1}. Let g ∈ S. Then g and ∏\ng′∈ S\\{ g} g′ are\ncoprime, so that the extended Euclidean algorithm yields po lynomials u, v with\nu ·g + v ·∏\ng′∈ S\\{ g} g′ = 1.\nDeﬁne eg := v ·∏\ng′∈ S\\{ g} g′. Then eg ≡ 1 mod g and eg ≡ 0 mod g′ for all g′ ∈ S \\ { g}. We\nhave\nT pk\n− T = ∏\ng∈ S g. (11)\nThe abstract reason is that both sides equal the product of al l T − u with u ∈ Fpk , but in\npractice, for ﬁxed p and k we can just verify this by expanding the product. It follows t hat\nthe images [eg] ∈ Fp[T ]/⟨T pk\n− T ⟩ are idempotent, pairwise orthogonal\n3, and their sum is 1.\nLet A be a pk-ring with p = 0, and let a ∈ A. We want to show that a is central. There is\na homomorphism of Fp-algebras\nFp[T ]/⟨T pk\n− T ⟩ → A\nmapping [T ] to a, and thus [eg] to eg(a). It follows that all the elements eg(a) ∈ A are\nidempotent, pairwise orthogonal, and their sum is 1, meaning\neg(a)2 = eg(a), e g(a) ·eg′(a) = 0 ( g ̸= g′), ∑\ng∈ S eg(a) = 1 . (12)\nFor an equational proof of these equations in A, we cannot use homomorphisms and quotients\nof polynomial rings, but we can just verify them by hand, only using the relation apk\n= a. In\norder to show that a = ∑\ng∈ S a ·eg(a) is central, it is then enough to show that each a ·eg(a)\ncommutes with each b ·eg(a), where b ∈ A and g ∈ S. Both elements stay the same when\nmultiplied with eg(a), which is central by Lemma 2.8. Hence, we may assume that eg(a) is the\nmultiplicative identity of our ring. In fact, we just have to replace every occurrence of 1 ∈ A\nbelow by eg(a), and the calculations remain valid. Of course, this corresp onds to the product\ndecomposition in our proof of Theorem 7.4, but what we do here is an equational version of\nthis argument.\n3Two idempotents e, f are called orthogonal if ef = 0.\n\n==================== PAGE 23 ====================\nEQUATIONAL PROOFS OF JACOBSON’S THEOREM 23\nStrictly speaking, we are not allowed to speak of “irreducib le polynomials” in an equational\nproof. The construction above, however, just explains the method how to write down an\nequational proof for a ﬁxed pair (p, k), in which we are just using the polynomial expressions\neg(a) and g(a) in A. This will become more clear in the examples below.\nSo, we have a polynomial g ∈ S with eg(a) = 1 , and this implies eg′(a) = 0 for g′ ̸= g.\nNext, we claim that\ng(a) = 0 (13)\nholds in A. To see this, notice that for all g′ ̸= g we have 1 = eg(a) ≡ 0 mod g′(a), i.e. g′(a)\nis a unit. On the other hand, by eq. ( 11) we have 0 = apk\n− a = ∏\ng′∈ S g′(a). Since all factors\ng′(a) with g′ ̸= g are units, we must have g(a) = 0 . Conversely, g(a) = 0 implies eg(a) = 1\nsince eg ≡ 1 mod g.\nThe whole preceding discussion was just about ﬁnding some g ∈ S with g(a) = 0 . This is\nthe only thing we will need from it in the following. In classi cal logic, this means that Fp[a]\nis either zero or a ﬁeld.\nLet m := deg( g). If m = 1 , then a = u ·1 for some u ∈ Fp and we are done. So assume\nm > 1 (and hence k > 1). Notice that we are not using the LEM here. What we are writing\ndown is one proof for every g ∈ S. When S = {T, T + 1, . . . }, we start with g = T , then\ng = T + 1, etc. But for linear polynomials the proof is easy. So we switc h directly to the next\npolynomials. Incidentally, this means that the proof is eas y for k = 1, namely exactly the one\nin Theorem 5.5.\nSince g is irreducible of degree > 1, the constant term of g is a unit in Fp. So clearly, a ∈ A\nis a unit. Since g is irreducible of degree m, we have g |T pm\n− T (this can be veriﬁed manually\nwith a polynomial division), so that apm\n= a by eq. ( 13).\nLet b ∈ A. We want to show that a and b commute. Choose an enumeration f0, f1, . . . , fpm− 1\nof the set Fp[T ]<m of polynomials over Fp of degree < m , starting with f0 = 0 . We deﬁne a\nsequence of elements b0, b1, b2, . . . , bpm in A recursively by\nb0 := b, b n+1 := abn − bna − fn(a)bn. (14)\nFor example, b1 = ab − ba, and the goal is to prove b1 = 0. We claim that\nbpm = 0. (15)\nUnfortunately, our proof is not equational, but it is constr uctive, and eq. ( 15) can be veriﬁed\nin every example of p, k, g manually as well, just by using g(a) = 0 (see the examples below).\nDeﬁne the map d : A → A by d(x) := ax − xa. As before, we prove dpm\n= d, only using\napm\n= a. The deﬁnition of bn becomes\nbn =\n(\n(d − fn− 1(a)) ◦ . . . ◦ (d − f0(a))\n)\n(b).\nHence,\nbpm =\n∏\nf ∈ Fp[T ]<m\n(d − f (a))(b),\nso that it suﬃces to prove the equation\nT pm\n− T =\n∏\nf ∈ Fp[X]<m\n(T − f (a))\nin A[T ]. Since g(a) = 0 , there is a homomorphism of Fp-algebras Fp[X]/⟨g(X)⟩ → A, [X] ↦→ a,\nand it suﬃces to prove\nT pm\n− T =\n∏\nf ∈ Fp[X]<m\n(T − [f ])\nin (Fp[X]/⟨g(X)⟩)[T ]. This follows from the fact that Fp[X]/⟨g(X)⟩ is a ﬁeld with pm elements,\nnamely those [f ] with f ∈ Fp[X]<m. This ﬁnishes the proof of eq. ( 15).\nNext, we claim that for n ≥ 1 there is an equational proof of\nbn+1 = 0 = ⇒ bn = 0. (16)\n\n==================== PAGE 24 ====================\n24 MARTIN BRANDENBURG\nIf bn+1 = 0 , this means bna = ( a − fn(a))bn = f ′(a)bn with f ′ := T − fn. Notice that f ′ has\ndegree < k since k > 1 and fn has degree < m ≤ k. By assumption, there is an equational proof\nof the constructive Wedderburn Theorem Wp,k,f ′, so that bna = abn, meaning fn(a)bn = 0 .\nWe claim that fn(a) is a unit in A, which leads to bn = 0 . In fact, fn and g are coprime\n(since fn ̸= 0 has degree < m = deg( g) and g is irreducible), so that ufn + vg = 1 for some\ncomputable polynomials u, v. Then 1 = u(a)fn(a) + v(a)g(a) = u(a)fn(a), proving our claim.\nCombining eq. (\n15) and eq. ( 16) gives b1 = 0, so that a, b commute. □\nRemark 7.6. If two polynomials g, g′ ∈ S only diﬀer by a linear substitution, then the proof\nfor g in Theorem 7.5 (that g(a) = 0 implies that a is central) leads to a proof for g′, so that g′\nmay be omitted. In fact, if g′ = w g(uT + v) for v ∈ Fp and u, w ∈ F×\np , then the assumption\ng′(a) = 0 means g(ua + v) = 0 . If there is a proof for g, then ua + v is central and hence a\ntoo. Similarly, if g, g′ are reciprocal polynomials (up to a unit), then the proof for g leads to\na proof for g′. In fact, if g′(a) = 0 , then a is a unit with amg(a− 1) = 0 , so that g(a− 1) = 0 .\nIf there is a proof for g, then a− 1 is central, so that a is central as well. Again, one can write\nsome SageMath code that computes a suﬃcient set of polynomia ls for us.\nWe will now show how the equational proof provided by Theorem\n7.5 looks like in the\nexample p = 2 and k = 2. It will be considerably more complicated than ours in Theor em 6.4,\nbut it explains the method (that works for all k ≥ 1) quite well.\nExample 7.7. Consider p = 2 and k = 2 . So we will show (again) that every 4-ring A is\ncommutative. We have S = {T, T + 1, T 2 + T + 1}. The extended Euclidean algorithm yields\nthe equations\nT 2 ·(T ) + 1 ·(T + 1)(T 2 + T + 1) = 1\n(T 2 + 1) ·(T + 1) + 1 ·T (T 2 + T + 1) = 1\n1 ·(T 2 + T + 1) + 1 ·T (T + 1) = 1\nTherefore, we deﬁne\neT := (T + 1)(T 2 + T + 1) = T 3 + 1,\neT +1 := T (T 2 + T + 1) = T 3 + T 2 + T,\neT 2+T +1 := T (T + 1) = T 2 + T.\nLet a ∈ A. Then the elements eT (a), eT +1(a), eT 2+T +1(a) are idempotent (hence central),\npairwise orthogonal, and their sum is 1. We can verify this by a direct calculation, just using\na4 = a (and of course 2 = 0 in A). We only show two examples: a3 + 1 is idempotent since\n(a3 + 1)2 = a6 + 1 = a3 + 1, and (a3 + 1)(a2 + a) = ( a4 + a)(a + 1) = 0( a + 1) = 0 . It suﬃces\nto prove that every a ·eg(a) commutes with all b ·eg(a), where g ∈ S and b ∈ A. For g = T\nwe ﬁnd\na ·eT (a) = a ·(a3 + 1) = a4 + a = 0,\nso this case is trivial. For g = T + 1 we ﬁnd\na ·eT +1(a) = a4 + a3 + a2 = eT +1(a),\nso this case is also trivial. Linear polynomials will always be trivial. The only interesting case\nis g = T 2 + T + 1. Here,\na ·eg(a) = a3 + a2.\nNow, we do not necessarily have g(a) = a2 + a + 1 = 0 as in eq. ( 13), but here is why we may\nassume it: A direct calculation shows\n(a3 + a2)2 + (a3 + a2) + (a2 + a) = 0 .\nSo c := a ·eg(a) satisﬁes c2 + c + 1 = 0 in the 4-ring eg(a)A with multiplicative identity eg(a),\nand it suﬃces to prove that c is central in eg(e)A. Formally, this ring does not even exist in\n\n==================== PAGE 25 ====================\nEQUATIONAL PROOFS OF JACOBSON’S THEOREM 25\nequational logic, but any equational proof in eg(e)A can be turned into an equational proof\nin A after we replace the multiplicative identity with eg(a). Alternatively, we may argue as in\nthe proof of Theorem 7.4 and assume 1 = eT 2+T +1(a) = a2 + a. So we will assume\na2 + a + 1 = 0\nfrom now on. We enumerate the polynomials f0, f1, f2, f3 over F2 of degree < 2 by 0, 1, T, T +1.\nIf b ∈ A, we deﬁne a sequence recursively by b0 := b and bn+1 := abn + bna + fn(a)bn. We have\nb0 = b, b1 = ab + ba and then\nb2 = ab1 + b1a + b1\n= a(ab + ba) + (ab + ba)a + (ab + ba)\n= a2b + ba2 + ab + ba (using a2 = a + 1)\n= (a + 1)b + b(a + 1) + ab + ba = 0.\nSo we are lucky, the sequence terminates quite early. (We onl y expected b4 = 0 from the\ngeneral proof in Theorem 7.5.) Assume that we can show\nax + xa = x =⇒ x = 0.\nThen we can derive b1 = 0 from b2 = 0 and we are done. This is exactly the constructive\nWedderburn Theorem W2,2,T +1. Let us prove it here: Assume ax + xa = x. We calculate\n(a + x)2 = a2 + ax + xa + x2 = a2 + (x + x2).\nSince x + x2 is idempotent, it is central. Hence, we can calculate\na + x = (a + x)4 = (a2 + (x + x2))2 = a4 + (x + x2)2 = a + x + x2.\nFrom this we conclude x2 = 0, i.e. x = 0, and we are done.\nOne could remove all references to the more general method fr om this proof, and also get\nrid of all remarks outside of equational logic. We will not do that here, also because it will be\nless clear what is actually happening (and why).\nExample 7.8. For p = 2 and k = 3 , one can proceed in a similar way, and we only brieﬂy\nindicate how it works (we give a better proof in a moment). It s uﬃces to consider g = T 3+T +1,\nsince the other irreducible polynomial of degree 3 is g(T +1). So we may assume a3 +a+1 = 0 .\nWe enumerate the polynomials fi of degree < 3 by 0, 1, T, T +1, T 2, T 2+1, T 2+T, T 2+T +1.\nDeﬁne the sequence bn recursively by b0 := b and bn+1 := abn + bna + fn(a)bn. Then a routine\ncalculation gives b7 = 0 (even though we only expected b8 = 0 ). Hence, if W2,3,f has been\nveriﬁed for all f of degree < 3, we get an equational proof that 8-rings are commutative.\nIn many examples, one already has bn = 0 for some n < p m. This means that the commu-\ntativity proof does not require all Wp,k,f . Choosing a diﬀerent enumeration of the fi will also\nhelp to reduce the number n with bn = 0 . For example, in Example\n7.8 we will get b3 = 0\nwith a diﬀerent enumeration. Actually, certain monomials a re always suﬃcient:\nTheorem 7.9. Let p ∈ P and k ≥ 1. Assume that for all 1 ≤ i < k there is an equational\nproof of the constructive Wedderburn Theorem Wp,k,T pi . Then there is an equational proof that\nevery pk-ring with p = 0 is commutative.\nProof. Let A be a pk-ring with p = 0, and let a, b ∈ A. We will tweak the proof of Theorem 7.5.\nLet us ﬁrst simplify the deﬁnition of bn a bit. The old deﬁnition was bn+1 = (a− fn(a))bn − bna.\nSo we might as well deﬁne\nb0 := b, b n+1 := bna − fn(a)bn, (17)\nand start the enumeration with f0 := T . Again, Wp,k,fn proves bn+1 = 0 = ⇒ bn = 0 for\nn ≥ 1 (using that fn(a) − a is a unit then), and we have b1 = ba − ab. Hence, our goal is to\n\n==================== PAGE 26 ====================\n26 MARTIN BRANDENBURG\nﬁnd an equational proof of bn = 0 for some n ≥ 1. As before, we may assume g(a) = 0 for\nsome monic irreducible polynomial g ∈ Fp[T ] whose degree m divides k.\nNow, even though the computation of bn takes place inside a ring that is non-commutative\na priori, we shall see now that it can be encoded by a computati on in a commutative ring. By\ninduction, it follows from eq. (\n17) that we can express bn as\nbn = ∑\ni,j λi,jaibaj\nwith λi,j ∈ Fp. We encode this with the commutative polynomial\nBn := ∑\ni,j λi,jX iY j ∈ Fp[X, Y ].\nMore formally, we deﬁne Bn ∈ Fp[X, Y ] recursively by\nB0 := 1, B n+1 := BnY − fn(X)Bn.\nThen bn results from Bn by replacing any monomial λX iY j with λaibaj. This describes a\nmap Fp[X, Y ] ↦→ A that is clearly additive (but not multiplicative). In parti cular, Bn = 0 will\nimply bn = 0 . Since Fp[X, Y ] is commutative, there is a very simple formula for Bn: With\nB0 = 1 and Bn+1 = (Y − fn(X))Bn we ﬁnd\nBn =\n∏\ni<n\n(Y − fi(X)). (18)\nConsider the ﬁnite ﬁeld K := Fp[X]/⟨g(X)⟩. (This concept is not equational, but wait for it.)\nThe irreducible polynomial g(Y ) ∈ Fp[Y ] ⊆ K[Y ] has a root in K, namely [X]. Basic ﬁnite\nﬁeld theory tells us that the roots are formed by the orbits un der the Frobenius. Thus, we\nhave g(Y ) = ∏\ni<m(Y − [X]pi\n) in K[Y ]. This means that in Fp[X, Y ] there is an equation of\nthe form\ng(Y ) =\n∏\ni<m\n(Y − X pi\n) + g(X) ·h (19)\nfor some h ∈ Fp[X, Y ]. For every speciﬁc choice of p, g, we can simply ﬁnd this equation by\ncalculating ∏\ni<m(Y − X pi\n), so that no ﬁeld theory is required. Deﬁne fi := T pi\nfor i < m ;\nthe rest of the polynomials do not matter anymore. Then eqs. ( 18) and ( 19) imply\ng(Y ) = Bm(Y ) + g(X) ·h.\nBut since g(a) = 0 , we then get bm = 0 . For a purely equational proof, when p, g are ﬁxed,\none can just compute bm = 0 directly, just by using g(a) = 0 (see the examples below). □\nExample 7.10. From Theorem 7.9 we get the following equational proof that 8-rings are\ncommutative, for which only Morita [ Mo78] gave an equational proof so far. We may assume\ng(a) = 0 for g := T 3 + T + 1. For any element b, we deﬁne a sequence recursively by b0 := b\nand bn+1 := bna + a2n\nbn. We calculate:\nb1 = ba + ab,\nb2 = (ba + ab)a + a2(ba + ab)\n= ba2 + aba + a2ba + a3b (use a3 = a + 1)\n= b + ab + ba2 + aba + a2ba,\nb3 = (b + ab + ba2 + aba + a2ba)a + (a + a2)(b + ab + ba2 + aba + a2ba)\n= ba + aba + ba3 + aba2 + a2ba2 + ab + a2b + aba2 + a2ba + a3ba\n+ a2b + a3b + a2ba2 + a3ba + a4ba\n= ba + ab + aba + ba3 + a2ba + a3b + a4ba (use a3 = 1 + a, a 4 = a + a2)\n= ba + ab + aba + b + ba + a2ba + b + ab + aba + a2ba\n= 0.\n\n==================== PAGE 27 ====================\nEQUATIONAL PROOFS OF JACOBSON’S THEOREM 27\nIf W2,3,T 4 holds, b3 = 0 implies a2b2 = 0 , hence b2 = 0 . If W2,3,T 2 holds, then b2 = 0 implies\n(a2 + a)b1 = 0, hence b1 = 0 and a, b commute. We will prove these constructive Wedderburn\nTheorems in the next section as part of more general results.\nWe can also replace the proof of b3 = 0 with a “commutative computation”, as explained in\nthe general proof in Theorem 7.9. Deﬁne polynomials Bn ∈ F2[X, Y ] recursively by B0 := 1\nand Bn+1 := BnY + X 2n\nBn, so that bn results from Bn by replacing any monomial X iY j with\naibaj. We compute\nB3 = (Y + X)(Y + X 2)(Y + X 4)\n= Y 3 + (X + X 2 + X 4)Y 2 + (X 3 + X 4 + X 6)Y + X 7\n= Y 3 + Xg (X)Y 2 + X 3g(X)Y + (X 4 + X 2 + X + 1)g(X) + 1\n≡ Y 3 + Y + 1 mod g(X)\n≡ 0 mod g(X), g(Y )\nand hence b3 = 0.\nExample 7.11. With Theorems 7.5 and 7.9 we get the following equational proof that every\n27-ring with 3 = 0 is commutative. (There seems to be no equational proof in the literature\nyet.) The list of irreducible polynomials over F3 whose degree divides 3 is\nT, T + 1, T + 2, T 3 + 2T + 1, T 3 + 2T + 2, T 3 + T 2 + 2, T 3 + T 2 + T + 2,\nT 3 + T 2 + 2T + 1, T 3 + 2T 2 + 1, T 3 + 2T 2 + T + 1, T 3 + 2T 2 + 2T + 2.\nWe may remove the linear polynomials and those polynomials w hich are linear substitutions\nof others. This leaves us with T 3 + 2T + 1 and T 3 + T 2 + 2. So we need to handle two cases.\nAssume ﬁrst that a3 +2a+1 = 0 . Let us choose an enumeration starting with T, T +1, T +2.\nWe calculate:\nb1 = ba − ab,\nb2 = (ba − ab)a − (a + 1)(ba − ab)\n= ba2 + aba + a2b − ba + ab,\nb3 = (ba2 + aba + a2b − ba + ab)a − (a + 2)(ba2 + aba + a2b − ba + ab)\n= ba3 + aba2 + a2ba − ba2 + aba − aba2 − a2ba − a3b + aba − a2b\n+ ba2 + aba + a2b − ba + ab\n= ba3 − a3b − ba + ab (use a3 = a − 1)\n= b(a − 1) − (a − 1)b − ba + ab = 0.\nThus, if W3,3,T +1 and W3,3,T +2 hold, we get b1 = 0, and we are done.\nNow assume a3 + a2 + 2 = 0 . Let us choose an enumeration starting with T, T 3, T 9. We\ncalculate (we omit the details):\nb1 = ba − ab,\nb2 = b2a − a3b2 = b2a − (1 − a2)b2\n= − b + ab − ba + a2b − aba + ba2 + a2ba,\nb3 = b3a − a9b3 = b3a − (1 − a + a2)b3 = 0.\nThus, if W3,3,T 3 and W3,3,T 9 hold, we get b1 = 0, and we are done. We will prove the required\nWedderburn Theorems in the next section.\nExample 7.12. Let us prove that 16-rings are commutative with Theorem\n7.9. (Again, there\nseems to be no equational proof in the literature yet.) The ir reducible polynomials over F2\n\n==================== PAGE 28 ====================\n28 MARTIN BRANDENBURG\nwhose degree divides 4 are\nT 2 + T + 1, T 4 + T + 1, T 4 + T 3 + 1, T 4 + T 3 + T 2 + T + 1.\nBut T 4 + T + 1 is reciprocal to T 4 + T 3 + 1, and (T + 1)4 + (T + 1)3 + 1 = T 4 + T 3 + T 2 + T + 1.\nHence, it is enough to consider the two polynomials T 2 + T + 1 and T 4 + T + 1.\nIf a2 + a + 1 = 0 , we proceed as in Example\n7.7. Namely, we ﬁnd that x := ab + ba satisﬁes\nxa = (1 + a)x. Assuming W2,4,T +1, we get x = 0 and we are done. Now let a4 + a + 1 = 0 .\nIf we deﬁne bn+1 := bna − a2i\nbn for i = 0 , . . . ,4, then a routine computation, which we omit,\nshows b4 = 0 . Assuming W2,4,2i, we get b1 = 0 and we are done. We will prove the required\nconstructive Wedderburn Theorems in the next section.\n8. Constructive W edderburn Theorems\nIn this section, we will study more closely the constructive Wedderburn Theorems Wp,k,f\nfrom Deﬁnition 7.2. Recall that they state that ba = f (a)b holds in a pk-ring with p = 0 only\nwhen ba = ab. Because of Theorem 7.5, it would be very desirable to ﬁnd equational proofs\nin general. Here, we will just cover some partial results and special cases. We also indicate\nhow the general case could theoretically be handled by a comp uter program. Even though we\nalready saw in Theorem 7.9 that it is enough to consider f = T pm\nfor 0 ≤ m < k , we will\nconsider arbitrary polynomials f ∈ Fp[T ] for the moment.\nLemma 8.1. If f ∈ Fp[T ] is constant, then there is an equational proof of Wp,k,f .\nProof. In a pk-ring A with p = 0 , assume ba = ub holds for some u ∈ Fp. Then, since u and\nb commute, we have b(a − u) = 0 . Since A is reduced, this implies (a − u)b = 0, i.e. ab = ub.\nThus, ab = ba. □\nFor a polynomial f in one variable, we denote by f ◦j the j-fold composition of f with itself.\nLemma 8.2. If f ∈ Z[T ] is a polynomial and the equation ba = f (a)b holds in a ring, then\nfor all i, j ∈ N we have\nb jai = f ◦j(a)i b j.\nMore generally, for every g ∈ Z[T ] we have\nb jg(a) = g(f ◦j(a)) b j .\nProof. The second equation follows from the ﬁrst. The proof of the ﬁr st is routine, so a sketch\nshould be enough. One ﬁrst proves the case j = 1 by induction on i. Then one proves the\ncase i = 1 by induction on j. Then one proves the general case by induction on i. □\nLemma 8.3. For u ∈ Fp there is an equational proof of Wp,k,T +u.\nProof. Assume that ba = (a + u)b holds in a pk-ring with p = 0. By Lemma 8.2, we get\nba = bpk\na = (a + upk)bpk\n= ab. □\nLemma 8.4. To give an equational proof of Wp,k,f , one may assume that a and b are units.\nProof. Let ba = f (a)b. Assume that there is an equational proof of ba = ab in case b is a\nunit. By Lemma 2.17 we can write b = ue, where u is a unit and e = bpk− 1 is idempotent,\nhence central. It suﬃces to prove that b and ea commute, since b commutes with (1 − e)a\nanyway (the products vanish). Consider the pk-ring eA with multiplicative identity e and the\nhomomorphism α : A → eA, x ↦→ ex. Then we get α(b)α(a) = f (α(a))α(b) in eA. If Wp,k,f\nhas been proven in eA, where b is a unit, then α(b) = b commutes with α(a) = ea and we are\ndone. We need to make this argument equational, though, and t his works as in the proof of\nTheorem 7.5. Observe that e is neutral for both ea and b. Any proof of Wp,k,f can be applied\n\n==================== PAGE 29 ====================\nEQUATIONAL PROOFS OF JACOBSON’S THEOREM 29\nto the elements ea and b and the multiplicative “pseudo-identity” e. Thus, b and ea commute,\nand we are done. The reduction to the case that a is a unit works exactly the same. □\nWhen b is a unit, then Wp,k,f states that ba = f (a)b implies f (a) = a. Thus, we need to\nshow that a is a ﬁxed point of f . We will see in a moment that at least a is a ﬁxed point of a\ncertain power f ◦d.\nDeﬁnition 8.5. Let f, g ∈ Fp[T ] and k ≥ 1. We write f ≡ k g or just f ≡ g (when k is\nclear from the context) when there is an equational proof of f (a) = g(a) for all elements a of\na pk-ring with p = 0 . The set of equivalence classes becomes a monoid with respec t to the\ncomposition of polynomials:\n(f, g) ↦→ f ◦ g.\nNotice that it is well-deﬁned, and the multiplicative ident ity is T . The underlying set can be\nidentiﬁed with the one of Fp[T ]/⟨T pk\n− T ⟩, since f ≡ g holds if and only if T pk\n− T |f − g.\nIn particular, this monoid is ﬁnite, it has exactly ppk\nelements.\nRemark 8.6. If f is an element of a ﬁnite monoid, whose multiplication we writ e as ◦, then\nthere must be a pair of numbers i ≥ 0 and π > 0 such that f ◦i = f ◦(i+π). Then the powers of\nf are eventually periodic:\n1, f, . . . , f◦i, f ◦(i+1), . . . , f◦(i+π− 1), f ◦i, f ◦(i+1), . . . .\nWe say that f is of period π and index i. If π is minimal, we call π the period, similarly for\nthe index.\nIn particular, every f ∈ Fp[T ] has some period and index with respect to the ﬁnite monoid\n(Fp[T ]/⟨T pk\n− T ⟩, ◦, T ) from Deﬁnition 8.5. Both can be eﬃciently computed for every speciﬁc\nchoice of p, k, f because of Lemma 2.9. See also the following example.\nExample 8.7.\n(1) Constant polynomials are of period 1 and index 1.\n(2) The polynomial − T is of period 2 and index 0 (also of period 1 when p = 2).\n(3) For p = 2 , k = 3 , f = T 2 + T , we compute f ◦2 = T 4 + T , f ◦3 = T 4 + T 2 and\nf ◦4 = T 8 + T 2 ≡ T 2 + T , so that f is of period 3 and index 1.\n(4) For p = 2 , k = 4 , f = T 2 + 1, we compute f ◦2 = ( T 2 + 1)2 + 1 = T 4, f ◦3 = T 8 + 1\nand f ◦4 = T 16 ≡ T , so that f is of period 4 and index 0.\nProposition 8.8. Let f ∈ Fp[T ] be a polynomial of period π > 0. Assume that a, b are two\nelements of a pk-ring with p = 0 with ba = f (a)b, and b is a unit. Then we have f ◦d(a) = a,\nwhere d := gcd(π, pk − 1). In particular, if d = 1, there is an equational proof of Wp,k,f .\nNotice that this result generalizes both Lemma 8.1 and Lemma 8.3, and that the assumption\nthat b is a unit is harmless because of Lemma 8.4. Without this assumption, we can only\nconclude that b commutes with ad.\nProof. Choose i ≥ 0 with f ◦i ≡ f ◦(i+π). Lemma 8.2 allows us to compute\nbibπa = bi+πa = f ◦(i+π)(a)bi+π = f ◦i(a)bibπ = biabπ.\nSince bi is a unit, this shows that a and bπ commute. But then\nf ◦π(a)bπ = bπa = abπ.\nSince bπ is a unit, we get\nf ◦π(a) = a. (20)\nNext, we compute\nf (a)b = ba = bpk\na = f ◦pk\n(a)bpk\n= f ◦pk\n(a)b.\n\n==================== PAGE 30 ====================\n30 MARTIN BRANDENBURG\nSince b is a unit, we get f (a) = f ◦pk\n(a). Apply f ◦(π− 1) to both sides and use a = f ◦π(a).\nThen we get\nf ◦(pk− 1)(a) = a. (21)\nIf d := gcd(π, pk − 1), then (\n20), ( 21) and the Bézout identity imply f ◦d(a) = a. □\nLemma 8.9. Let m ∈ N and let π be the order of [m] in the additive group Z/kZ. Then the\nmonomial T pm\nis of period π and index 0.\nProof. Let f := T pm\n. Write πm = sk with s ∈ N. Then f ◦π = T pmπ\n= T psk\n≡ T . □\nTheorem 8.10. Let p ∈ P and k ≥ 1. If gcd(k, pk − 1) = 1 , then there is an equational proof\nthat every pk-ring with p = 0 is commutative.\nProof. This follows from Theorem 7.9, Proposition 8.8 and Lemma 8.9. □\nRemark 8.11. For the lack of a better name, let us call k ≥ 1 good for p ∈ P if we have\ngcd(k, pk − 1) = 1 ,\notherwise bad for p. With Theorem 8.10 we have solved the equational commutativity problem\nfor good exponents, so let us analyze how many numbers are goo d. For p = 2 , the 22 bad\nnumbers k ≤ 100 are\n6, 12, 18, 20, 21, 24, 30, 36, 40, 42, 48, 54, 60, 63, 66, 72, 78, 80, 84, 90, 96, 100.\nFor the other 78 numbers k ≤ 100, we thus have an equational proof that 2k-rings are com-\nmutative. As before, this then leads to many more cases: By The orem 4.1, every 94-ring is\na 2lcm(1,2,5) = 2 10-ring, hence commutative. For p > 2, every good number must be odd.\nFor p = 3 and p = 5 , most odd numbers are also good. For example, the only odd num bers\nk ≤ 100 that are bad for 3 are 39, 55, and the only odd numbers k ≤ 100 that are bad for 5\nare 55, 93. But for p > 5 there are much more bad odd numbers. If k |k′ and k is bad, then\nk′ is bad as well, since gcd(k, pk − 1) |gcd(k′, pk′\n− 1).\nThe following characterization of bad numbers has been foun d by Max Alekseyev.\nLemma 8.12. Let p ∈ P. The set of bad numbers k for p is equal to the union\n⋃\nq∈ P, q̸=p\nq ·ordq(p) ·N+.\nHere, ordq refers to the order in the group (Z/qZ)× .\nProof. Let k ≥ 1. If k belongs to the union, there is a prime q ̸= p with q ·ordq(p) |k. Then\nq |k and q |pk − 1, so that gcd(k, pk − 1) > 1. Conversely, if gcd(k, pk − 1) > 1, there is a prime\nq with q |k and q |pk − 1. The second condition implies q ̸= p and then becomes equivalent\nto ordq(p) |k. Since the order divides q − 1, it is coprime to q. Hence, q ·ordq(p) |k. □\nWith Theorem 8.10, 26 and 34 are the only prime powers ≤ 100 for which we don’t have an\nequational commutativity proof yet, see also Table 1. For the moment, the general case seems\nto be out of reach. But it turns out that Wp,k,f can be veriﬁed by a computer program, at\nleast theoretically, since it is a ﬁnite problem:\nProposition 8.13. There is a ﬁnite Fp-algebra Up,k,f with a concrete ﬁnite presentation such\nthat Up,k,f is commutative if and only if Wp,k,f holds.\nProof. Because of Lemma 8.4, it suﬃces to work with units. (This is not necessary for the\nproof, but it will decrease the dimension of the algebra belo w.) We deﬁne the Fp-algebra by\nthe ﬁnite presentation Vp,k,f := Fp⟨X, Y : X pk− 1 = 1, Y pk− 1 = 1, Y X = f (X)Y ⟩. We saw in\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
