{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_52","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' results/proof_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Proof Notes for the \\(k=3\\) Case\n\n## Lemma 1: Split-Polynomial Centrality\n\nLet \\(A\\) be a reduced ring and let \\(c\\in A\\). Suppose\n\\[\n\\prod_{\\lambda\\in \\Lambda}(c-\\lambda)=0\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\Lambda\\subseteq \\mathbb F_p\\) and the elements of \\(\\Lambda\\) are distinct. Then \\(c\\) is central.\n\nProof. For each \\(\\lambda\\in\\Lambda\\), Lagrange interpolation gives a polynomial \\(e_\\lambda(T)\\in\\mathbb F_p[T]\\) such that \\(e_\\lambda(\\mu)=\\delta_{\\lambda,\\mu}\\) for all \\(\\mu\\in\\Lambda\\). Modulo \\(\\prod_{\\mu\\in\\Lambda}(T-\\mu)\\), the polynomials \\(e_\\lambda\\) are pairwise orthogonal idempotents and \\(\\sum_\\lambda e_\\lambda=1\\), \\(T=\\sum_\\lambda \\lambda e_\\lambda\\). Evaluating at \\(c\\), the elements \\(e_\\lambda(c)\\) are idempotents. Idempotents are central in a reduced ring, so \\(c=\\sum_\\lambda \\lambda e_\\lambda(c)\\) is central. \\(\\square\\)\n\nIn particular, if \\(p\\equiv 1\\pmod 3\\) and \\(c^3=1\\), then \\(c\\) is central, because \\(T^3-1\\) splits into distinct linear factors over \\(\\mathbb F_p\\).\n\n## Lemma 2: Finite Norm Section\n\nLet \\(p\\equiv 1\\pmod 3\\), let \\(g\\in\\mathbb F_p[T]\\) be irreducible of degree \\(3\\), and let \\(i\\in\\{1,2\\}\\). Put \\(q=p^3\\). In the finite reduced algebra\n\\[\nB=\\mathbb F_p[S,T]/(S^{q-1}-1,\\ g(T)),\n\\]\nwith automorphism \\(\\sigma_i(S)=S\\), \\(\\sigma_i(T)=T^{p^i}\\), there is a unit \\(U\\in B\\) such that\n\\[\nU\\,\\sigma_i(U)\\,\\sigma_i^2(U)=S.\n\\]\n\nProof. Decompose \\(C=\\mathbb F_p[S]/(S^{q-1}-1)\\). Since the roots of \\(S^{q-1}-1\\) are exactly \\(\\mathbb F_q^\\times\\), every simple factor of \\(C\\) is either \\(\\mathbb F_p\\) or \\(\\mathbb F_q\\). Over a factor \\(\\mathbb F_p\\), the algebra \\(B\\) is the cubic field \\(\\mathbb F_q\\), and the norm \\(\\mathbb F_q^\\times\\to\\mathbb F_p^\\times\\) is surjective. Over a factor \\(\\mathbb F_q\\), the polynomial \\(g\\) splits and \\(B\\cong\\mathbb F_q^3\\), while \\(\\sigma_i\\) cyclically permutes the three factors; the norm map \\((x_0,x_1,x_2)\\mapsto x_0x_1x_2\\) is again surjective. Therefore \\(S\\) has a norm preimage on every simple factor. Combining these preimages by the Chinese remainder theorem gives \\(U\\in B\\). \\(\\square\\)\n\nFor fixed \\(p\\) and \\(g\\), the element \\(U\\) is represented by a concrete polynomial \\(U_{g,i}(S,T)\\). Verifying the displayed norm identity in \\(B\\) is a finite polynomial calculation, hence gives an equational certificate.\n\n## Proposition 3: Monomial Wedderburn for \\(p\\equiv 1\\pmod 3\\)\n\nLet \\(p\\equiv 1\\pmod 3\\) and \\(q=p^3\\). Then \\(W_{p,3,T^p}\\) and \\(W_{p,3,T^{p^2}}\\) have equational proofs.\n\nProof. It is enough to treat \\(W_{p,3,T^{p^i}}\\) for \\(i=1,2\\). Let \\(A\\) be a \\(q\\)-ring of characteristic \\(p\\), and suppose\n\\[\nba=a^{p^i}b.\n\\]\nBy Brandenburg's unit reduction for \\(W_{p,k,f}\\), it suffices to prove the claim on the support where \\(b\\) is a unit. Decompose the element \\(a\\) by the central idempotents attached to the irreducible factors of \\(T^q-T\\). Linear factors give \\(a^{p^i}=a\\), so \\(ba=ab\\) there.\n\nNow work on an irreducible cubic component \\(g(a)=0\\). The element \\(a^{p^i}-a\\) is a unit on this component because \\(g\\) and \\(T^{p^i}-T\\) are coprime. We show that a unit \\(b\\) satisfying \\(ba=a^{p^i}b\\) makes the component zero.\n\nLet \\(\\sigma(a)=a^{p^i}\\). Then \\(\\sigma^3(a)=a\\), and\n\\[\nb^3a=a^{p^{3i}}b^3=ab^3,\n\\]\nso \\(s=b^3\\) commutes with \\(a\\); it also commutes with \\(b\\). By Lemma 2, choose \\(u\\) in the commutative subring generated by \\(a\\) and \\(s\\) such that\n\\[\nu\\,\\sigma(u)\\,\\sigma^2(u)=s.\n\\]\nSince \\(b u=\\sigma(u)b\\), the element \\(c=u^{-1}b\\) satisfies\n\\[\nc^3=u^{-1}\\sigma(u)^{-1}\\sigma^2(u)^{-1}b^3=1.\n\\]\nBecause \\(p\\equiv 1\\pmod 3\\), Lemma 1 makes \\(c\\) central. But\n\\[\nca=u^{-1}ba=u^{-1}a^{p^i}b=a^{p^i}c.\n\\]\nSince \\(c\\) is central and a unit, \\(a=a^{p^i}\\). This contradicts the cubic component unless the identity element of the component is zero, because \\(a^{p^i}-a\\) is a unit. Hence the \\(b\\)-unit support on every cubic component is zero. Therefore \\(ba=ab\\) on all components.\n\nAll ingredients are finite polynomial identities for fixed \\(p\\): the idempotents for the factorization of \\(T^q-T\\), the Bezout inverse of \\(T^{p^i}-T\\) modulo each cubic \\(g\\), the norm-section identity of Lemma 2, and the idempotent decomposition of \\(T^3-1\\). Thus the proof is equational in Brandenburg's sense. \\(\\square\\)\n\n## Theorem 4: \\(k=3\\) Commutativity\n\nFor every prime \\(p\\), every characteristic-\\(p\\) ring satisfying \\(x^{p^3}=x\\) is commutative, and for each fixed \\(p\\) the proof can be written equationally.\n\nProof. If \\(p=2\\), \\(p=3\\), or \\(p\\equiv 2\\pmod 3\\), then \\(\\gcd(3,p^3-1)=1\\), so Brandenburg's period criterion applies. If \\(p\\equiv 1\\pmod 3\\), Proposition 3 proves the two monomial constructive Wedderburn statements \\(W_{p,3,T^p}\\) and \\(W_{p,3,T^{p^2}}\\). Brandenburg's monomial sufficiency theorem then gives commutativity. \\(\\square\\)\n\n## Status\n\nThe argument above gives a proof schema and identifies the finite certificates needed to materialize an explicit equational derivation for any chosen prime \\(p\\equiv 1\\pmod 3\\). The computational scripts in `src/` verify the congruence split and test the first open case \\(p=7\\) in the skew-algebra model.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
